- The constitution itself codifies CPV supremacy.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Constitution of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam","publisher":"Thu Vien Phap Luat (English consolidated text)","publication_date":"2025-01-01","url":"https://thuvienphapluat.vn/van-ban/EN/Bo-may-hanh-chinh/Integrated-document-52-VBHN-VPQH-2025-The-Constitution-of-the-Socialist-Republic-of-Vietnam/667918/tieng-anh.aspx?tab=7","claim":"Article 4 states that the Communist Party of Vietnam is the force leading the State and society, that it is accountable to the people for its decisions, and that its organizations and members operate within the framework of the Constitution and law.","locator":"Article 4","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary constitutional text, albeit in consolidated English translation, and directly relevant to political contestability and party dominance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Structural baseline in force during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Structural, not personal; it defines the party-state framework Trọng controlled as CPV General Secretary.","contrary_evidence":["This is a self-describing constitutional clause, not proof that accountability was effective in practice.","The text does not create actual multi-party competition."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.4","4B.5","4B.8"]}

- Freedom House describes a one-party state with blocked competition and weak accountability.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Vietnam: Freedom in the World 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022-01-01","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/vietnam/freedom-world/2022","claim":"Freedom House says Vietnam is a one-party state dominated by the Communist Party of Vietnam, that independent candidates are technically allowed but most are banned in practice, that the CPV controls elections, and that opposition parties are not legally allowed.","locator":"overview and Political Rights sections","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Comparative country report with direct election and party-system assessment for the target period.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Current baseline carried into 2022; some examples refer to 2021 but describe the system in force during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong; this is the institutional environment under Trọng's leadership.","contrary_evidence":["The report is synthesized rather than case-file evidence.","It mixes 2021 events into a 2022 country profile."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.3","4B.8","4B.10"]}

- Freedom on the Net shows direct censorship and surveillance tools in use.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Vietnam: Freedom on the Net 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022-01-01","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/vietnam/freedom-net/2022","claim":"Freedom House reports that Vietnam is a one-party state where independent candidates are mostly banned in practice, officials ordered international platforms to remove thousands of items, new regulations tightened online content restrictions, and the expansion of government apps and a central ID database raised privacy concerns.","locator":"overview and Key Developments, June 1, 2021 - May 31, 2022","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialized internet-freedom report with concrete 2022-relevant censorship and data-control findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly spans the 2022 target window.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong; these controls were imposed by the party-state Trọng led.","contrary_evidence":["It is an advocacy report, so the summary should be cross-checked against official legal texts and Reuters reporting.","It does not isolate which leader ordered each measure."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- Reuters reported a 24-hour takedown plan for illegal social-media content.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Vietnam plans 24-hour take-down law for illegal social media content -sources","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-04-20","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/exclusivevietnam-plans-24hour-takedown-law-for-illegal-social-media-content-sources-2806667","claim":"Reuters reported that Vietnam was preparing rules requiring social media firms to remove illegal content within 24 hours and block illegal livestreams within three hours, strengthening the ruling Communist Party's ability to crack down on anti-state activity.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting based on people with direct knowledge, but accessed through a republisher.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect to Trọng through the party-state apparatus; not a direct personal order transcript.","contrary_evidence":["The piece reports draft/planned rules, so it shows intended control rather than final implementation.","It does not identify Trọng as the individual drafting the rule."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}

- Reuters reported on onshore data storage and local-office requirements.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Vietnam orders tech firms to store user data onshore","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-18","url":"https://tech.yahoo.com/general/articles/vietnam-orders-tech-firms-store-065638137.html","claim":"Reuters reported that Vietnam ordered technology firms to store users' data locally and set up local offices, and that authorities would be able to request data and content removal under the new decree.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting with clear policy detail, though hosted on a syndication page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect to Trọng via the party-state's cybersecurity and information-control apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["This is a state-control measure, not evidence of broader political inclusion.","The report does not prove personal direction by Trọng."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}

- Reuters reported a new basis for controlling who can post news on social platforms.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Vietnam preparing rules to limit news posts on social media accounts - sources","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/quote/stock/META-PLATFORMS-INC-10547141/news/Vietnam-preparing-rules-to-limit-news-posts-on-social-media-accounts-sources-41883636/","claim":"Reuters reported that Vietnam was preparing rules to limit which social-media accounts can post news-related content, creating a legal basis for controlling news dissemination on Facebook and YouTube.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on a live policy tightening, albeit through a republisher.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect to Trọng through the party-state information apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["The article describes a proposed or preparing rule, not a fully observed enforcement record.","It does not attribute the proposal to Trọng personally."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across constitutional texts, Freedom House, State Department, RSF, HRW, Amnesty, Reuters, and Vietnamese state media.
- I retained the sources that directly speak to the chapter: party supremacy, noncompetitive elections, independent media suppression, digital controls, and term-limit/succession context.
- I rejected duplicate mirrors of the same Reuters reports, plus generic anti-corruption or economic items that do not materially bear on political freedom.
- I set aside broader human-rights case reports already used in Chapter 3B when they did not add new 4B-specific evidence beyond repression generally.
- A few pages were useful only as snippets or syndication copies; I kept the best accessible version with the same underlying Reuters claim.
- I did not use the client matrix.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct public evidence of the internal 2022 decision path is thin: most of the record is structural, not a transcript of Trọng personally directing repression.
- The strongest positive evidence is formal, not substantive: the constitution says the CPV is accountable and the state has legal procedures, but the empirical record shows those constraints were weak.
- I did not find any credible 2022 evidence of opposition victories, real alternation, or an independent legislature checking the CPV.
- More search would mainly help if you want internal party minutes, succession documents, or closed judicial/disciplinary records. Publicly, the 2022 baseline already looks saturated and consistently authoritarian.

